  <title>
   <narrative xml:lang="EN">Social and Economic development of Northern Sierra Leone Communities</narrative>
  </title>
  <description>
   <narrative xml:lang="EN">The civil war in Sierra Leone ended in 2002 and with four years of support from a Comic Relief-funded project, many communities in the north are now engaged in economic development activities and the potential for entrepreneurship and small businesses is huge. However, poverty is still widespread. Young people, PWDs and women are economically and politically marginalized due to unemployment, poverty and bad governance. Many artisans and petty traders have rehabilitated their businesses, but are operating on a very small scale due to inability to meet credit requirements to expand them. The follow-on project will reach more communities and support small businesses to improve and expand. It will strengthen and work with CBOs and through them target young people, petty traders (with a focus on women), PWDs and artisans to provide them with micro-credit loans, support apprenticeship and train beneficiaries in numeracy and literacy, business and financial management. This will create employment opportunities, promote entrepreneurships and make small businesses more sustainable. The CBOs will be linked with advocacy networks to lobby and hold government and chiefs accountable. As a result, small businesses will thrive, employment opportunities will increase especially for marginalized groups, and poverty will be reduced in the area.</narrative>
  </description>
